Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Rotate the array one step to the right, k times.
- In each rotation, store the last element, shift all elements one position right, and place the last element at the beginning.

Algorithm:
1. Compute k = k % n.
2. Repeat k times:
   - Store last element.
   - Shift all elements right by one.
   - Place stored element at index 0.

Time Complexity: O(n × k)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        k %= n;

        while (k--) {
            int last = nums[n - 1];

            for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; i--)
                nums[i] = nums[i - 1];

            nums[0] = last;
        }
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Create a temporary array.
- Every element at index i moves to (i + k) % n.
- Copy the temporary array back to nums.

Algorithm:
1. Compute k = k % n.
2. Create temp array of size n.
3. Place nums[i] at temp[(i + k) % n].
4. Copy temp into nums.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        k %= n;

        vector<int> temp(n);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            temp[(i + k) % n] = nums[i];

        nums = temp;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
Idea:
- Use the Reversal Algorithm.
- Reverse the entire array.
- Reverse the first k elements.
- Reverse the remaining n-k elements.
- This achieves rotation in-place.

Algorithm:
1. Compute k = k % n.
2. Reverse the entire array.
3. Reverse indices [0, k-1].
4. Reverse indices [k, n-1].

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        k %= n;

        reverse(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        reverse(nums.begin(), nums.begin() + k);
        reverse(nums.begin() + k, nums.end());
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- Brute force performs k rotations, making it inefficient for large k.
- Using an extra array gives linear time but requires O(n) extra space.
- The Reversal Algorithm is the expected optimal solution in interviews.
- Why does the reversal algorithm work?
  - Reverse the whole array.
  - The last k elements come to the front (but reversed).
  - Reverse the first k elements to restore their order.
  - Reverse the remaining n-k elements to restore their order.
- Edge Cases:
  - k = 0.
  - k > n (always use k %= n).
  - n = 1.
  - Empty array (if allowed).
- Follow-up:
  - Can you rotate left by k positions?
    - Reverse first k elements.
    - Reverse remaining elements.
    - Reverse the entire array.
- The reversal technique is a classic interview pattern and is useful in many array/string problems.
